The binary is at /workspace/executable (a code-snapshot generator, default theme "candy"). Working dir: use /tmp/cstheme (create it). Copy /tmp/cs/png.py into /tmp/cstheme (it provides `load(path)->(w,h,RGBA bytearray)` and `px(img,w,x,y)`).

Basic usage: `/workspace/executable -f FILE -o OUT.png --silent --scale-factor 3` renders a code snapshot PNG with syntax highlighting. Code area is dark (40,42,54). Text ink pixels are colored per token type. To find the color of a token, render a file containing that token, locate the glyph ink pixels (pixels inside the window that are NOT the window background (40,42,54) and not anti-aliasing of the background), and take the most saturated/dominant solid color (sample the pixel at the center of a thick glyph stroke; or take the most common non-background color within the ink region). Render at scale-factor 3 for cleaner sampling. Window top-left is at (246,246) px at scale 3; first code line ink is roughly y in [375,405], x starting ~294.

TASK: extract the "candy" theme's syntax-highlighting palette. For each language below, create a small source file with tokens of each category, render, and report the RGB color for each category. Categories: keyword, string, comment, number/numeric literal, function name, type/class name, variable/identifier (default text), punctuation/operators, constant/builtin, attribute/decorator, preprocessor/macro. Report per-language token->RGB mappings.

Languages & sample files:
1. Rust (test.rs): `// comment
