def foo(x):
    return "str" + str(123)
class Bar:
    pass
@deco
async def g(): ...
` — colors of comment, keyword `import`/`def`/`return`/`class`, string, number `123`, builtin func `str`, function `foo`, class `Bar`, decorator `@deco`.
3. JavaScript (test.js): `// c
const x = 42;
function f(a) { return `tpl${a}`; }
let s = "hi";
console.log(f);
` — keyword `const`/`function`/`return`, number, string, template string, builtin `console`, function `f`.
4. Plaintext (no extension, file named 'plain'): `hello world 123` — default text color.
5. Also with `-l rust` flag vs .rs extension — confirm same colors.

Also: 6. Determine how the language is auto-detected: create files with extensions .py .js .ts .rs .go .c .cpp .java .rb .sh .json .toml .md .html .css .yaml .txt and unknown .zzz, each containing the single line `x = "s" // c` (or `# c` comment where applicable); report which ones highlight strings/comments (color differs from default text) and which render as plain text. Just report per-extension: string color, comment color observed (or 'plain').

7. Check whether a file with no extension but a shebang `#!/bin/bash
echo hi` gets highlighted.

Report everything as compact structured notes (lists of token=RGB). These colors will be hardcoded into a reimplementation, so accuracy of the RGB values matters most; approximate token categorization is fine.

